I don't know whether this is your problem or not, but
not all cdroms (e.g. the Digital Unix operating system ones I've got)
are cdfs filesystems, some have Unix file systems on.
In this case you have to mount them using a command like

  # mount -r /dev/rz4c /mnt

If that's not it I'm not sure, but trying something like

    dd if=/dev/rz4c of=/dev/null

would give you an idea of whether the disk/drive is working and 
you're just having a mount problem, or whether there's something
wrong with getting bytes off the disk at all.


