>>>>> "Thomas" == Thomas Bates <bates@atlantis.its.dist.maricopa.edu> writes:

    Thomas> 	We're currently running C2 on most of our machines,
    Thomas> with a few running 'standard' Digital UNIX.
	
    Thomas> 	We'd like the ability of running crack 5 on our C2
    Thomas> machines, just to doublecheck that the passwords, even
    Thomas> though they passed the enhanced security check, are
    Thomas> secure.

    Thomas> 	Does anyone have any information on how to get the
    Thomas> [hash in the same routine?] passwords out of the C2
    Thomas> database?


	The code below works on my system (DEC/OSF v 2.0).  You run it
(as root) and it outputs a file called "translate.out" on which you can
run Crack5.  Basically, it reads the protected password database and
creates a normal /etc/passwd style file.

	It still isn't quite right.  Not being a professional, I
didn't have time to figure out the details of the protected password
database.  It works most of the time and once in a while it crashes
(without doing any damage, it just doesn't work).  I guess I'm saying
"use at your own risk"

	Good Luck.


							-Adam

------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
  code for generating an ordinary /etc/passwd file from the
   C2 one
*/

#include <pwd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/security.h>
#include <prot.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    char *filename="translate.out";
    FILE *pwfile;
    struct passwd *next_passwd;
    struct pr_passwd *prot_passwd;
    int good=1;
    if (!(pwfile=fopen(filename,"w")))
    {
        printf("Error opening output file.  Exiting.\n");
        exit(1);
    }
    set_auth_parameters(); /* initialize database interface */
    setpwent(); /* initialize ordinary database */
    while (good)
    {
        next_passwd = getpwent(); /* get an entry from /etc/passwd */
        if (next_passwd==NULL) good=0;
        else
            {
		/* now get the protected entry for the same UID */
                prot_passwd=getprpwuid(next_passwd->pw_uid); 
		/* put the crypted password field into the /etc/passwd style */
                next_passwd->pw_passwd=prot_passwd->ufld.fd_encrypt; 
		/* write that entry to the output file */
                putpwent(next_passwd, pwfile);
            }
        fflush(pwfile);
    }
    endprpwent(); /* close protected database */
    endpwent();   /* close ordinary database */
}
