In article <610dse$11lk$1@nntp6.u.washington.edu> merritt@u.washington.edu (Ethan A Merritt) writes:

[ Want to use 'ufs' filesystem to transport data between DIGITAL UNIX
  machines at work and RedHat Linux 4.2 at home, using ZIP disks.
  However, all attempts fail. Why? Solutions? ]

OK. Forgive my naming, not sure it I am 100% accurate on these...

The 'ufs' supported by Linux is the Sun 'UNIX file system', while
Digital uses the 'ULTRIX file system'. So unfortunately, they are
different. I don't believe there are any UNIX filesystems common
to both.

If the DU systems have the "DOS tools" subset on them (OSFDOSTOOLS4xx)
you may be able to mount and use a DOS filesystem. DU uses 'mformat'
to format the disk, then 'mcopy', 'mdir', ... (you get the idea).
Only unknown is if DU can handle a filesystem that large.

See the mtools(1) man page for details on using the tools.

The other choice is to dump the data as a tar file to the raw device,
then untar it on the other machine. I have done this to move data on
other operating systems; you should have no problems.

